const title = "How Long Do Dishwashers Last? — Same Day";
const description = "How long a dishwasher lasts: about 9 years — shorter than most kitchen appliances. Why LA's hard water is the big factor, the honest repair-vs-replace math, and how to add years.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How long does a dishwasher last on average?",
    a: "About 9 years, per the NAHB home-component life-expectancy data. That's noticeably shorter than most kitchen appliances — a range or refrigerator often runs half again as long. It's an older reference (originally 2007), so treat it as a general benchmark, but the short number is real: dishwashers combine water, heat, detergent, and food debris in one machine, which is hard on pumps, seals, and the heating element. In hard-water areas like Los Angeles, that 9-year figure is optimistic unless the machine is maintained."
  },
  {
    q: "Why does hard water shorten a dishwasher's life in Los Angeles?",
    a: "Southern California water runs 12 to 18 grains per gallon, and that mineral load is a dishwasher's biggest enemy. Calcium and magnesium scale up the heating element, coat the spray-arm holes so they spray weakly, clog the filter and pump housing, and leave the cloudy film you see on glasses. Every one of those makes the machine work harder and wear faster. It's the main reason dishwashers in LA tend to reach the shorter end of their lifespan — and the main reason regular cleaning and rinse aid matter more here than almost anywhere."
  },
  {
    q: "Is it worth repairing a dishwasher?",
    a: "It depends on the age and the part. A typical dishwasher repair runs roughly $100 to $300, occasionally more for a bigger job. Because the machine's expected life is only around 9 years, the math tightens sooner than with other appliances. Our rule of thumb: if it's under about 6 or 7 years old, most repairs — a drain pump, a valve, a filter, a door latch — are clearly worth it. If it's already 8 or more years old and needs a major part like the circulation pump or the control board, replacement often makes more sense, and we'll tell you honestly. The $89 diagnostic gives you the real numbers first."
  },
  {
    q: "What usually fails first on a dishwasher?",
    a: "In our experience across LA, the drain path leads — a clogged filter or a worn drain pump causing standing water at the bottom of the tub. Next come the spray arms and the wash pump (scaled up or weakened by hard water), the door latch and gasket, and the heating element. Many of these are early, affordable repairs if caught before they cascade. A dishwasher that suddenly won't drain, won't clean, or leaks is worth a look before the underlying cause takes out a second part."
  },
  {
    q: "How can I make my dishwasher last longer?",
    a: "Fight the hard water and keep the debris out. Clean the filter at the bottom of the tub regularly — most people don't know it exists, and a clogged one strains the pump. Run a dishwasher-cleaner or plain white-vinegar cycle monthly to clear scale. Use rinse aid, which genuinely helps against LA's minerals. Scrape plates rather than heavily pre-rinsing (modern detergents need some soil to work against), don't overload so the spray arms can turn freely, and run the hot-water tap at the sink briefly before starting so the cycle begins hot. Those habits routinely add years here."
  }
];

