<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">About 9 years — and hard water is the reason it's not more.</h2>

    <p><strong>A dishwasher lasts about 9 years on average, per the NAHB home-component life-expectancy data — noticeably shorter than a range or refrigerator. It's an older benchmark (originally 2007), so treat it as general, but the short number holds up: a dishwasher combines water, heat, detergent, and food debris in one machine, which is hard on the pumps, seals, and heating element. In Los Angeles, the deciding factor is hard water — 12 to 18 grains per gallon of mineral load that scales up the heater, spray arms, and pump. Maintained well, an LA dishwasher reaches that 9-year mark; neglected, it falls short. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>Because the expected life is shorter, the repair-or-replace decision arrives sooner than with other appliances — which makes an honest read on the machine's age and the actual repair cost more important, not less. That's exactly what our diagnostic is for.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 WHAT AFFECTS IT -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · What decides where yours lands</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Hard water first, then usage and care.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>LA's hard water (the big one)</h3>
      <p>This is the factor that moves the number most here. Calcium and magnesium scale up the heating element, coat the spray-arm holes so they wash weakly, and clog the filter and pump housing. Every bit of that makes the machine strain and wear faster, and it's why LA dishwashers tend to land at the short end without regular cleaning.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>How often it runs</h3>
      <p>A dishwasher run daily ages faster than one run a few times a week. Overloading also hurts — jammed racks stop the spray arms from turning freely and strain the wash pump.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Maintenance</h3>
      <p>Cleaning the filter, running a monthly cleaner or vinegar cycle to clear scale, and using rinse aid all add real years in hard water. Skipping them gives those years back quickly.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>How it's loaded and used</h3>
      <p>Heavily pre-rinsing (modern detergents need some soil), overpacking, and running cold all reduce cleaning performance and push the machine harder. Small habits, measurable difference.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Brand plays a role, but the most-cited reliability data comes from one regional dealer's first-year service rates, not national failure rates, and it doesn't cover every brand — so it's directional, not a guarantee. We won't tell you a badge buys you a decade in hard water.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 REPAIR VS REPLACE -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Repair or replace?</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The math tightens sooner on a dishwasher.</h2>

    <p>A typical dishwasher repair runs roughly <strong>$100 to $300</strong>, occasionally more for a bigger job. Because the expected life is only about 9 years, the decision line sits earlier than with longer-lived appliances:</p>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Usually worth repairing</h3>
      <p>Under about 6 or 7 years old, with a common fault — a drain pump, water inlet valve, filter, door latch, or gasket. These are affordable and the machine has real life left.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Weigh it carefully</h3>
      <p>Already 8+ years old and facing a major part — the circulation (wash) pump or the control board — where the repair climbs toward the price of a new mid-range unit. That's where replacement often wins, and we'll say so plainly.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The most common call we get here is standing water — a dishwasher that won't drain — and it's usually the cheap end (a clogged filter, often no parts at all) rather than the machine being done. See our <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-draining/">dishwasher not draining</a> guide and typical pricing on <a href="/price-list/dishwasher-repair-cost/">dishwasher repair costs</a>. For a brand-specific take, our blog covers <a href="/blog/bosch-dishwasher-worth-repairing/">whether a Bosch dishwasher is worth repairing</a>. <strong>$89 diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 EXTEND LIFE -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · Get more years out of it</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Beat the hard water.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Clean the filter regularly.</strong> The cylindrical filter at the bottom of the tub — most people don't know it's there, and a clogged one strains the pump and leaves standing water.</li>
      <li><strong>Run a monthly cleaner cycle.</strong> A dishwasher cleaner or plain white vinegar clears the scale that LA's hard water leaves on the heater and spray arms.</li>
      <li><strong>Use rinse aid.</strong> It genuinely helps against mineral film and improves drying.</li>
      <li><strong>Scrape, don't heavily pre-rinse.</strong> Modern detergents need some soil to work against; don't overload so the spray arms turn freely.</li>
      <li><strong>Start it hot.</strong> Run the kitchen hot tap briefly before starting so the cycle begins at temperature.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Our blog goes deeper on this exact problem: <a href="/blog/la-hard-water-killing-dishwasher/">how LA's hard water is killing your dishwasher</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 WHEN TO CALL + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Standing water, not cleaning, or leaking? Get it looked at.</h2>

    <p>A dishwasher that won't drain, stops cleaning, or leaks is telling you a wear part has gone — and on a machine with a shorter life to begin with, catching it early is the difference between a modest repair and buying new. Our techs diagnose the fault, clear the hard-water scale where it's the culprit, quote the real number, and give you a straight repair-or-replace read for your machine's age.</p>

    <p>More help: the full <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/">dishwasher repair</a> hub, and the common failures — <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-draining/">not draining</a>, <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-cleaning/">not cleaning</a>, <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/leaking-water/">leaking water</a>, and <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/error-codes/">error codes</a>. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/bosch-dishwasher-repair/">Bosch</a>, <a href="/brands/ge-dishwasher-repair/">GE</a>, and <a href="/brands/whirlpool-dishwasher-repair/">Whirlpool</a> dishwasher repair. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dishwasher-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/dishwasher-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/dishwasher-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong> <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Dishwasher lifespan: common questions.</h2>

